---
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SKILL.md.example
# This is the template for a skill. Skills are reusable knowledge artefacts.
# Copy this folder to skills/<your-skill-name>/SKILL.md, edit, then run
# `make register` to update manifest.json.
#
# Universal frontmatter fields — all optional unless noted. The manifest
# generator validates this; bad YAML or undeclared presets fail register.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Identifier. Defaults to the parent directory name if omitted.
# Convention: lowercase-with-hyphens.
name: example-skill

# One-line description. Surfaced in `ai-toolkit list` and flows into
# tool-side frontmatter via {description} substitution (Cursor's
# description field, Copilot's instruction description, etc.).
description: One-line description of what this skill is for.

# Optional. Maintainer or author. Informational only.
author: your-name

# Optional. Presets this skill is bundled into. Every name listed here
# MUST be declared in config/presets.json or `make register` will fail
# with "references unknown preset".
presets:
  - quality-gates
  - skill-development

# Optional. Allowlist of tools this skill applies to. When set, the
# install resolver SKIPS this skill for any tool not in this list and
# logs a per-tool warning. Omit (most common case) and the skill installs
# for every tool whose `supportedAssets` includes `skills`.
tools:
  - claude-code
  - cursor
  - vscode-copilot

# Optional. Per-tool frontmatter overrides. The values here win over the
# tool config's frontmatter template in config/tools.json. Each key
# corresponds to a tool name; the inner object is merged into the
# rendered destination frontmatter.
#
# Common use cases:
#   - Scope a Cursor rule with globs and alwaysApply.
#   - Scope a VS Code Copilot instruction with applyTo.
#   - Pick a model for a Cursor subagent or Copilot custom agent.
#
# Only the keys you want to override are needed; everything else falls
# back to the tool config's defaults.
overrides:
  cursor:
    alwaysApply: false
    globs: "**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}"
  vscode-copilot:
    applyTo: "src/**"
  # any-other-tool:
  #   any-tool-specific-key: value
---

# Example Skill

The body of the file is the skill's content — what the AI will read.
Treat this section like a short, self-contained reference doc.

## When to use this skill

Open with a one-line trigger. Be specific. "Invoke this when X" reads
better than "this is about X".

## When not to use it

Equally important. Skills that don't honestly call out their non-use
cases get over-triggered and start polluting unrelated conversations.

## Procedure / Content / Checklist

Whatever shape the skill needs. Markdown is fine. Keep the body short
and readable; if you need long-form references, scripts, or examples,
put them in adjacent folders (see "Multi-folder skills" below).

## Gotchas

- **Names with whitespace or punctuation in frontmatter strings.** YAML
  doesn't require quoting in most cases, but `description: foo: bar`
  WILL break the parser. Quote with single quotes if the value contains
  a colon: `description: 'foo: bar'`.
- **`presets` references must be declared first.** The generator runs in
  one pass; if you list a preset that doesn't exist in
  `config/presets.json`, registration fails. Add to `presets.json` first.
- **`tools` filter doesn't make the asset stop existing.** It's an
  install-time allowlist. The skill still appears in `ai-toolkit list`;
  it just gets skipped for tools not in the list. Use `ai-toolkit list
  --tool <name>` to see the install-time filtered view.
- **`overrides.<tool>` merges, doesn't replace.** Setting
  `overrides.cursor.globs` overrides only that one key; the rest of
  Cursor's frontmatter template (description, alwaysApply) still
  applies. Set every key you want to differ from the default.
- **Body content is what the tool actually loads.** Frontmatter is for
  metadata; long-form prose belongs in the body. For Cursor `.mdc` files
  and Copilot `.instructions.md` files the body is wrapped in a fresh
  tool-shape frontmatter at install time — the ai-toolkit metadata
  (name, presets, author, tools, overrides) never leaks through.

## Multi-folder skills (optional)

A skill folder can carry adjacent material the body references:

  skills/<name>/
  ├── SKILL.md          ← this file
  ├── eval.json         ← optional: deterministic test cases
  ├── scripts/          ← runnable helpers the skill cites
  ├── references/       ← long-form references
  └── assets/           ← binary or non-text resources

For tools whose destination is a directory (Claude Code, Antigravity,
Gemini CLI), the entire tree is copied. For tools whose destination is
a single flat file (Cursor `.mdc`, Copilot `.instructions.md`), only
the `SKILL.md` is extracted and the adjuncts are intentionally not
carried over — those formats have no equivalent.

See docs/guides/adding-an-asset.md and docs/eval-format.md for the
multi-folder + eval workflow.

